## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical sorting algorithm design that balances cache efficiency with worst-case performance guarantees. Hybrid approaches like this are commonly used in real-world systems where both average-case speed and theoretical bounds matter.

## Key Concepts
- **Hybrid sorting**: Combines partitioning (QuickSort) with stable merging (MergeSort)
- **Introsort-like depth limiting**: Prevents pathological cases
- **In-place stable merging**: Achieved through array rotations
- **Cache-friendly recursive structure**: Larger partitions processed first
- **Complexity**: Average/worst case O(n log n), additional space O(log n) for recursion stack

## Code Implementation
```javascript
function hybridSort(arr, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1, depth = 0) {
  const n = right - left + 1;
  const threshold = 32;
  const maxDepth = 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(n));

  if (n <= 1) return;

  if (n <= threshold || depth >= maxDepth) {
    // Bottom-up in-place stable merge sort
    mergeSortInPlace(arr, left, right);
    return;
  }

  // QuickSort with median-of-three pivot
  const pivotIndex = medianOfThree(arr, left, right);
  const partitionIndex = partition(arr, left, right, pivotIndex);

  // Recurse on larger partition first to bound stack depth
  const leftSize = partitionIndex - left;
  const rightSize = right - partitionIndex;

  if (leftSize > rightSize) {
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
  } else {
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
  }
}
```

